### SA Health Clinical Incident Management Policy

#### **Incident Management**
Health services must have systems in place for managing clinical incidents, including:
- **Reporting**: All clinical incidents must be reported and documented[2](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/about+us/governance/policy+governance/policies/clinical+incident+management+policy)[1](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/89e269804e341fb5b45ffcc09343dd7f/Patient+Incident+management+and+OD_final.pdf?MOD=AJPERES).
- **Reviewing**: Regular reviews of incident data to identify trends and areas for improvement [2](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/about+us/governance/policy+governance/policies/clinical+incident+management+policy) [1](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/89e269804e341fb5b45ffcc09343dd7f/Patient+Incident+management+and+OD_final.pdf?MOD=AJPERES).
- **Open Disclosure**: Informing patients and their families about incidents that have affected their care and the steps taken to prevent recurrence [2](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/about+us/governance/policy+governance/policies/clinical+incident+management+policy) [1](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/89e269804e341fb5b45ffcc09343dd7f/Patient+Incident+management+and+OD_final.pdf?MOD=AJPERES).

#### **Incident Reporting for Mental Health Clients**
- **Adverse Events**: Any incident that results in harm to a mental health client must be reported and documented [3](https://www.health.wa.gov.au/About-us/Policy-frameworks/Clinical-Governance-Safety-and-Quality/Mandatory-requirements/Clinical-Incident-Management-Policy).
- **Near Misses**: Incidents that could have resulted in harm but did not must also be reported [3](https://www.health.wa.gov.au/About-us/Policy-frameworks/Clinical-Governance-Safety-and-Quality/Mandatory-requirements/Clinical-Incident-Management-Policy).

#### **SA Health Open Disclosure Policy**
- **Communication**: Providing clear and honest information about the incident to patients and their families [4](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/about+us/governance/policy+governance/policies/how+to+conduct+open+disclosure+guideline).
- **Support**: Offering support and assistance to the client and their family [4](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/about+us/governance/policy+governance/policies/how+to+conduct+open+disclosure+guideline).

#### **Internal Reporting Systems**
- **Health Service Policies**: Individual health services may have their own internal policies and procedures for reporting incidents. These are designed to complement state-wide policies and ensure comprehensive incident management [5](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/9f94b97c-fc07-4a2e-a8d8-dae44e3ebeaf/How+to+Conduct+Open+Disclosure+Guideline.pdf?MOD=AJPERES).

#### **Clinical Governance, Safety, and Quality**
- **Incident Reporting**: All clinical incidents, including adverse events and near misses, must be reported and documented to ensure continuous improvement in patient safety and care quality [6](https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/sites/default/files/2021-09/open-disclosure-principles-elements-and-process_9.pdf).

These requirements ensure comprehensive documentation, transparency, and accountability within the healthcare system, aiming to improve patient safety and care quality.

